a narrative beyond what I was being told.

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:54.920
And I see what's happening
in the industry now.

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:55.940
- Yeah.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:59.963
- And that is because of you
making that brave choice.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.440
So thank you for that.

00:26:03.440 --> 00:26:05.320
- Thank you, oh my goodness.

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:07.270
Hearing what you just said like, I mean,

00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:09.197
I'm trying not to cry right now.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:15.110
But like, it's just so meaningful

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.680
because it's easy for me to say, like,

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.690
of course you shouldn't
work with him, you know,

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:21.020
he's a jerk, he's a monster,

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:26.020
but I just find it an incredibly brave

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:29.310
and incredibly generous

00:26:32.131 --> 00:26:33.381
that you would say to me,

00:26:36.091 --> 00:26:38.780
that my story and what I went through

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:45.020
was important enough to reconsider that.

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.480
- Absolutely.

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:51.480
And how hard was it,

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:54.040
for you to engage in this document?

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:57.310
In which I really wanna
make a caveat about

00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:59.730
that your family did not produce.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:02.010
- No, no, they came to us.

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:06.170
- I think that's very important
for people to understand

00:27:06.170 --> 00:27:08.740
your family did not
produce this documentary.

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:10.290
- No, we participated.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:14.010
And apparently everyone
was asked to participate.

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:17.030
- Yeah, no, they went to everyone

00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:21.150
and that was important, you know,

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:24.520
even just to me to know
that like that there was

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:27.120
a level of journalistic
integrity at work here.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.170
- So did your family get together and say,

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:31.850
these people are different,
they're taking a tone,

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:32.960
they're using a diligence.

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:36.940
There's a thoroughness
here that feels safer

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.633
in order to come out and
make this brave decision.

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:46.060
- I was actually the one
who initially told my mom

00:27:46.830 --> 00:27:49.880
and told Ronan, you know,
like, Hey, I sat down

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:52.840
with these people and I really think that

00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:54.350
they are on the up and up.

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:58.770
So then when my mom
agreed to be interviewed,

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.880
I was really, really shocked.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:04.500
And when Ronan agreed,
I was even more shocked.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:06.543
And then Fletcher agreed,

00:28:07.711 --> 00:28:12.100
I mean, I was picking my jaw
up off the floor and, you know,

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.460
gradually more and more
of my siblings signed on

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:18.020
and a few of them didn't
and I've spoken to them

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:18.900
about that too.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.323
And I respect that decision as well.

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:25.923
But I mean, in terms
of like family meeting,

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:31.650
not really, but there was communication.

00:28:31.650 --> 00:28:34.860
And interestingly, the
documentary has led to

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:38.720
greater communication between
us as a result, I think,

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:40.180
which is interesting.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:44.460
- May I ask why you were
surprised that they signed on?

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:48.470
- I think mostly because
we wouldn't talk about it

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:49.303
to each other.

00:28:49.303 --> 00:28:52.743
So talking about it publicly,

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:59.413
it just seemed absolutely
incomprehensible.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:05.563
- How did your mom feel
watching the documentary?

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:09.270
- I mean, it was tough
because I would have loved

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.003
to have been able to
watch the series with her,

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:17.704
and at the time that just wasn't possible.

00:29:17.704 --> 00:29:20.893
We were in separate quarantine
pods, unfortunately.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:27.460
So I really sort of
heard everything from her

00:29:27.460 --> 00:29:29.860
after the fact, you know,

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:33.000
it was just a very strange pathway

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:36.993
that we navigated separately and together,

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:42.630
but I think it definitely
changed a lot about

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:47.760
how me and my mom relate to each other

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:49.280
and interact with each other.

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:53.700
And I think there's a very renewed level

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:59.143
of just respect, just woman to woman.

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:05.200
- I mean, sometimes when I think back on

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:10.100
memories of my life, there
has like a gauze, a patina,

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:13.273
a sort of surrealist reverie.

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:18.370
I know mentally that time has gone by,

00:30:18.370 --> 00:30:23.050
but I also can feel
like I can be back there

00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:25.253
in a moment's notice like that.

00:30:26.638 --> 00:30:31.310
Even if it does feel almost
watching a home movie.

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:36.310
And then when you were
watching the home movie of you,

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:40.963
what did you think and feel and remember?

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:46.010
- It was a really bizarre,

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:48.420
psychological experience for me, honestly,

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:53.420
because I had the video in my house.

00:30:56.350 --> 00:30:58.370
I gave it to my husband and I just said,

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.290
put it into sort of
like the darkest corner

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.470
that you can find, just know where it is.

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:07.700
And that moment of just
in case happened when

00:31:07.700 --> 00:31:12.440
I was talking to Amy and she asked if

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:15.750
she could see slash use
the video and the series.

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:18.530
And my first reaction was like,

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:21.030
absolutely not like, no,
I draw the line here.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:24.210
This is too much, it is too personal.

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:25.490
I don't wanna see the video.

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:27.990
I don't want anybody
else to see this video.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:32.990
And Amy, I don't know how
she talked me into it.

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:36.580
She managed to get the video taken.

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:40.730
I had my husband bring it
out of that dark corner,

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.760
wherever he stashed it in the closet.

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:48.760
And we watched it sitting in my house

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:51.250
and I just suddenly burst into tears.

00:31:51.250 --> 00:31:55.040
And it hit me everything
from the tape, just,

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.030
I mean, it punched me right in the face

00:31:58.030 --> 00:32:01.690
and I was just sobbing uncontrollably

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:06.200
for like a couple of days on and off,

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:08.710
about what I saw and just sort of

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.570
trying to mentally

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:18.620
reconcile the fact that

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:22.113
that little girl on the tape was me.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:28.160
And she was talking about
something that happened to me

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:32.477
and it was overwhelming.

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:39.420
It's very difficult sometimes

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:43.790
to know that, that part of me,

00:32:43.790 --> 00:32:48.790
is such a dark,

00:32:49.490 --> 00:32:52.140
painful place, I mean.

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:53.570
'Cause when I look at my life now,

00:32:53.570 --> 00:32:56.030
I have a wonderful husband,
I have a wonderful daughter,

00:32:56.030 --> 00:32:59.490
I have a wonderful life and
I'm really excited about,

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:03.220
you know, my career writing and, you know,

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:05.680
taking my daughter to
kindergarten next year

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:10.680
and all the wonderful
things that surround me.

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:15.320
And yet there is this
impenetrable darkness,

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:18.913
deep, deep inside that,

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:20.980
I mean, I'm gonna be honest.

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:23.183
I still don't know what to do with.

00:33:24.020 --> 00:33:24.853
- Wow.

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:31.010
How do you deal with any fears
that crop up as a parent?

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:34.120
'Cause I have mine from my
own history or my own stories

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:37.160
or things that are big triggers to me.

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:40.280
- I mean, I think about that a lot because

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:43.230
I do have things that
trigger me in my daily life.

00:33:43.230 --> 00:33:46.360
Things that, you know,
are probably just like

00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:48.580
completely stupid for other people,

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:53.300
that just send me
completely down the spiral.

00:33:53.300 --> 00:33:54.850
And I'm actually kind of proud of myself

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:57.310
that I've gotten to the point where I can

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:03.780
see a trigger or experience
some sort of trigger, I freeze

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:06.860
and all that fun stuff happens.

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:11.860
The emotions, the recall, the sensations

00:34:12.310 --> 00:34:16.230
and I can process it.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:20.770
And sometimes it means waiting through

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:22.530
some of the negativity.

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:26.330
- How do you tell people
to handle their triggers,

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:28.783
if you see an image?

00:34:31.015 --> 00:34:34.460
- I mean, this happened to
me, like, was it last week

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:36.260
maybe the week before,
I was just, you know,

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:41.260
scrolling through my newsfeed
and an article pops up that,

00:34:43.720 --> 00:34:47.403
you know, who and his
wife went to a restaurant.

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:53.490
And I was just tailspin completely.

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:54.960
I was just like, I can't handle this.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:55.860
I don't wanna see this.

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:57.730
Like, why are they gonna
restaurants anyway?

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:02.730
Like I was really very heavily triggered

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:06.040
and I was not okay.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:11.040
And it took a couple of days

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:13.490
of me just sort of like

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:20.027
trying to like massage myself
back into a normal space.

00:35:20.027 --> 00:35:22.950
- Can I ask, you know,

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:26.850
if people looking in who
maybe have not experienced

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:29.470
anything like this, is it the layers,

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:31.460
the repetition, the many years,

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:33.590
you watch the documentary and...

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:39.300
- I mean, the unfairness
of it, it's frustrating.

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:42.143
And then it makes me feel hopeless.

00:35:43.259 --> 00:35:44.497
Like, what was the point of it all?

00:35:44.497 --> 00:35:49.497
And then it's reminding
myself of facts that I know,

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:54.950
things that I can tether to.

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:58.480
The documentary did
accomplish something, I mean,

00:35:58.480 --> 00:36:01.520
I think RAINN reported that
people were calling them

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:05.470
at an increased rate of about
20% during the documentary,

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:08.740
which means that survivors are out there

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:12.670
connecting with my story
and healing from it.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:15.010
It's bittersweet thinking, you know,

00:36:15.010 --> 00:36:20.010
like this story is something that connects

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:26.903
a lot of survivors who are hurting,

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:31.510
and okay, what more can we do for them?

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:34.110
Like how can we make

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:39.790
this world a space that
women feel empowered

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:44.680
or feel at least less terrified?

00:36:44.680 --> 00:36:46.900
- That is so well said, thank you.

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:50.460
It really is, what a
perfect, amazing answer

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.170
to that question.

00:36:52.170 --> 00:36:53.150
- It's tough because like,

00:36:53.150 --> 00:36:54.853
I wish that I could say like,

00:36:56.655 --> 00:37:00.530
here is like a 10 step plan to, you know,

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:03.310
completely feeling healed
and fulfilled in your life.

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:04.633
If you follow these steps,

00:37:05.797 --> 00:37:07.097
you're gonna be just fine.

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:09.180
I wish I had that.

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:12.750
What works for me might
not work for somebody else.

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:17.660
So I think the best advice that I offer

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:20.750
is just be true to yourself

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.343
and the truth that you have lived.

00:37:28.440 --> 00:37:31.850
- I think that's really good advice.

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:33.134
I really do.

00:37:33.134 --> 00:37:34.520
(audience applauding)

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:37.130
I couldn't be more impressed by how Dylan

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:39.870
has handled herself, her history,

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:43.360
and even the criticism
aimed at her for decades.

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:47.130
Her resilience is a complete inspiration.

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:48.567
I'll be right back.

00:37:48.567 --> 00:37:51.150
(upbeat music)

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:02.610
Welcome back, it's time for Drew's News.

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:06.010
A very special, very
precocious Drew's news.

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:07.560
Because in season one,

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:11.600
we introduced you to our
adorable seven-year-old

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:15.523
kid reporter, Emmy Eaton, take a look.

00:38:16.430 --> 00:38:20.170
We'd love to look forward to
the next generation of newsers.

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:23.870
And in that spirit, we
have a very special guest.

00:38:23.870 --> 00:38:27.070
She is a budding young
journalist from Idaho Falls.

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:31.470
Who's becoming an internet
sensation for her interviews.

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:35.100
She's interviewed her mom,
her dad, her neighbors,

00:38:35.100 --> 00:38:39.430
Keith Morrison, Lester
Holt, Doctor Oz, oh, my God.

00:38:40.940 --> 00:38:44.110
She is a rising star,
and today, guess what?

00:38:44.110 --> 00:38:47.350
Rossi we get to join
that illustrious list.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:48.630
- I'm so honored.

00:38:48.630 --> 00:38:50.230
- Please welcome seven year old,

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:54.670
behind the seventh questions
with Emmy, Emmy Eton.

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:55.503
- Emmy.

00:38:55.503 --> 00:38:56.837
(audience cheering)

00:38:56.837 --> 00:38:57.670
- Hey.

00:38:59.648 --> 00:39:02.920
- I'm so excited to be
talking with you guys today.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:07.700
- Oh, my goodness Emmy,
you've done over 40 interviews

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:10.280
and you've got such a natural talent.

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:15.220
What is it about interviewing
that you like so much?

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:18.520
- I like that I get to
talk to so many people

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:21.947
that are so amazing and
I love asking questions.

00:39:21.947 --> 00:39:23.790
- Do you do research?

00:39:23.790 --> 00:39:25.460
Do you pick your subjects?

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:27.030
What's your process?

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:29.960
- Well, I think of what they do.

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:32.710
Like if I were interviewing an author,

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:35.190
I would think, oh, what
book do they write?

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:37.010
And I would ask about that.

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:40.410
And if they like write
a fantasy, I would say,

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:43.180
okay, why did you wanna write a fantasy?

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.013
- I love it.

00:39:44.013 --> 00:39:46.230
Have you always been curious
your whole entire life?

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:49.060
- Yes, I've been very curious.

00:39:49.060 --> 00:39:51.070
- That's why you're a
good journalist, Emmy.

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:54.570
- Is there a story that you
would like for everyone to hear

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:56.823
today that you feel is important?

00:39:58.560 --> 00:40:03.330
- There was this person,
she lives in Rexburg.

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:07.860
She made an igloo, they
call it the Skittle igloo.

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:09.500
They put pans of ice,

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:12.140
and then they all sued coloring

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:14.370
and they stuck it on the igloo.

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:16.960
There, the average they put lights in

00:40:16.960 --> 00:40:18.800
so it looks really cool at night.

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:20.500
And the dad's a doctor,

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:23.820
so every day after delivering a baby,

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:26.780
he would get back to here on the igloo.

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:29.970
- Okay, Emmy, I just
wish you the very best,

00:40:29.970 --> 00:40:32.680
and I want you to go out
there and keep asking

00:40:32.680 --> 00:40:34.460
the important questions,

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:36.900
showing the fun things
that are happening in life.

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:40.110
And most importantly, would
you come back and visit us?

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:40.970
- Yeah.

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:44.960
- Okay, good, I'm so
glad, we need more Emmy.

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:47.510
The world needs more Emmy.

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:50.080
Thank you so much for coming today.

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:51.600
- Keep up the great work, Emmy.

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:53.930
- Thank you too.

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:57.260
- Just cute, she's so cute and wonderful.

00:40:57.260 --> 00:40:59.040
Do go anywhere we've got more

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:01.820
seasoned two secrets coming up.

00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:02.874
That's right.

00:41:02.874 --> 00:41:05.457
(upbeat music)

